Package: SimLev
Type: Package
Title: Simulation and Backtesting with German Investment Taxation
Version: 0.8.0
Authors@R: person("ChemicalStats", "von der Mauerstrasse",
                  role = c("aut", "cre", "cph"),
                  email = "chemicalstatsy@protonmail.com")
Description: A fast/slow-path backtesting engine for leveraged and unleveraged ETF and
    multi-asset strategies. Supports calendar- and event-based rebalancing,
    dollar-cost averaging, asset splits, and a detailed model of German
    investment taxation including Vorabpauschale, Teilfreistellung and the
    loss-offset rules of sections 20 and 23 of the EStG. Further tools
    backcast product histories from base indices, costs and financing rates,
    run bootstrap studies of strategies over simulated index and leveraged
    product paths, and simulate retirement withdrawal rules. Research tools
    cover parameter searches with overfitting diagnostics (probability of
    backtest overfitting, deflated Sharpe ratio, walk-forward selection), data
    and portfolio preparation, and dependence analysis (copulas, tail
    dependence, DCC, quantile regression, CoVaR) conditional on user-supplied
    state variables such as inflation or interest rate spreads. Tactical
    allocation (rule-based switching, momentum rotation, dual momentum,
    canary protection, volatility targeting, glide paths), portfolio
    optimisation (minimum variance, maximum Sharpe, risk parity, hierarchical
    risk parity, minimum CVaR, resampling), risk decomposition and synthetic
    bond, money market and currency-hedged series complete the toolkit.
    Version 0.5 adds order fees, church tax and annual realisation of gains
    up to the tax allowance to the engine, scenario analysis (entropy
    pooling, copula stress scenarios, crisis replay), drawdown optimisation,
    robust covariance estimators, combinatorial purged cross-validation,
    multiple-testing haircuts, Sharpe ratio and reality-check tests, signal
    analysis, hidden Markov regimes, multi-country bootstraps, user-defined
    retirement horizons and goal-based dynamic programming. Performance-critical
    parts are implemented in C++ and C.
